      "text": "Given that \\(\\cos R = \\frac{4}{9}\\) in the right triangle \\(QRS\\), we need to find the length of \\(QS\\).\n\nFirst, recall that in a right triangle, the cosine of an angle is the ratio of the adjacent side to the hypotenuse. Here, \\(RQ\\) is the adjacent side to angle \\(R\\) and \\(QR\\) is the hypotenuse. Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\cos R = \\frac{RQ}{QR} = \\frac{4}{9}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(RQ = 4k\\) and \\(QR = 9k\\) for some positive constant \\(k\\). Since \\(QR\\) is the hypotenuse, by the Pythagorean theorem, we have:\n\n\\[\nQR^2 = RQ^2 + QS^2\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the known values, we get:\n\n\\[\n(9k)^2 = (4k)^2 + QS^2\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n81k^2 = 16k^2 + QS^2\n\\]\n\nSubtracting \\(16k^2\\) from both sides, we get:\n\n\\[\n65k^2 = QS^2\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides, we find:\n\n\\[\nQS = \\sqrt{65}k\n\\]\n\nWe are given that the length of \\(RS\\) is 9, so \\(QR = RS + QS\\). Therefore:\n\n\\[\n9k = 9 + \\sqrt{65}k\n\\]\n\nSubtracting \\(9k\\) from both sides, we get:\n\n\\[\n9k - 9 = \\sqrt{65}k\n\\]\n\nFactoring out \\(k\\) on the left side, we get:\n\n\\[\n9(k - 1) = \\sqrt{65}k\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(k\\), we divide both sides by \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\n9(1 - \\frac{1}{k}) = \\sqrt{65}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k = 1\\) would make \\(QR\\) and \\(RQ\\) undefined in the context of the triangle, we solve for \\(k\\) by isolating it:\n\n\\[\n9(1 - \\frac{1}{k}) = \\sqrt{65}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n9 - \\frac{9}{k} = \\sqrt{65}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n9 - \\sqrt{65} = \\frac{9}{k}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nk = \\frac{9}{9 - \\sqrt{65}}\n\\]\n\nTo rationalize the denominator, we multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n\\[\nk = \\frac{9(9 + \\sqrt{65})}{(9 - \\sqrt{65})(9 + \\sqrt{65})} = \\frac{9(9 + \\sqrt{65})}{81 - 65} = \\frac{9(9 + \\sqrt{65})}{16} = \\frac{81 + 9\\sqrt{65}}{16}\n\\]\n\nNow, we can find \\(QS\\):\n\n\\[\nQS = \\sqrt{65}k = \\sqrt{65} \\cdot \\frac{81 + 9\\sqrt{65}}{16} = \\frac{\\sqrt{65}(81 + 9\\sqrt{65})}{16} = \\frac{81\\sqrt{65} + 9 \\cdot 65}{16} = \\frac{81\\sqrt{65} + 585}{16}\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of \\(QS\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{81\\sqrt{65} + 585}{16}}\n\\]",
